*Switching to HTTP:**\n- Rewrite entire client/server communication\n- Handle multi-round-trip tool execution\n- Re-implement streaming\n- Update all tests\n- Migration path for existing users\n\n**Complexity: HIGH** (2-3 weeks to implement + test)\n\n---\n\n## Hybrid Approach: HTTP + WebSocket for Tools\n\n**Idea:** Best of both worlds?\n\n```typescript\n// Main flow: HTTP with SSE\nPOST /agent/chat (streaming)\nHeaders: { Authorization: Bearer token }\n\n// Tool execution: Separate WebSocket channel\nws://server/tools\n→ Only used for bidirectional tool calls\n```\n\n**PROS:**\n✅ Standard HTTP auth for main API\n✅ WebSocket only for complex tool flows\n✅ Simpler auth (HTTP headers)\n\n**CONS:**\n❌ **Two protocols to manage** (complexity!)\n❌ Two connections to maintain\n❌ More points of failure\n❌ Harder to debug\n❌ Confusing mental model\n\n**Verdict:** Probably worse than either pure approach.\n\n---\n\n## My Recommendation\n\n### **Keep WebSockets with Auth in Payload**\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\n1. **The auth problem is small** - Adding auth to message payload is a **simple solution** (few hours of work)\n\n2. **The protocol change is massive** - Switching to HTTP is a **major architectural change** (weeks of work, breaking changes, migration complexity)\n\n3. **Use case fit** - use-ai is fundamentally a **bidirectional, real-time chat system**. This naturally fits WebSocket.\n\n4. **Performance matters** - Multi-tool workflows would suffer **significant latency** with HTTP round trips\n\n5. **User experience** - WebSocket provides the **real-time feel** users expect from AI chat\n\n6. **AG-UI alignment** - While HTTP works, the protocol's event-streaming model fits WebSocket more naturally\n\n### **When to Reconsider HTTP:**\n\nConsider switching **only if**:\n- You need serverless deployment (no persistent connections)\n- You're building a public API (REST-first)\n- Tool calls become rare (<10% of interactions)\n- You need to work in WebSocket-restricted environme